The field shipped STAMPED BUT UNREAD. Every reference to silence_reason was
producer-side -- the two stamping chokepoints, the schema generator, the axis
registration -- and nothing read it back. That is the defect this project
already has a rule for ("a disclosure nothing reads is not one"), and it is the
same shape as the finding the previous commit documented about
validate_pass_dependencies: shipped, and wired to nothing.

It also discharged the wrong half of the ruling. The owner's 2026-09-11 decision
kept the recorded-reason work specifically ON THE OBSERVABILITY COMPLAINT, and a
value nobody is shown is not observability -- "an agent could grep the JSON" is a
weaker claim than the complaint asked for.

So `hypergumbo survey` now prints one line at the end of a run, beside the
validator's [warn] summary and under the same discipline (one line, stderr,
silent when every pass produced output):

  [passes] 57 of 78 emitted nothing: no_candidate_files=52, unreported=5 \
(per-pass detail in analysis_runs[].silence_reason)

`summarize_silence` / `format_silence_summary` / `emit_silence_summary` in
hypergumbo_core.pass_silence are the reusable consumer API behind it, so the
next consumer branches on the axis instead of re-deriving counts from
analysis_runs.

THREE CHOICES THAT ARE EACH AN AVOIDED TRAP:

  * A value OUTSIDE the vocabulary is counted under its own name, never dropped
    or bucketed into a neighbour. Dropping it would make a DRIFTED PRODUCER LOOK
    LIKE A CLEAN RUN -- exactly the failure this axis was declared to prevent.
  * Ordering is DETERMINISTIC (most-common first, ties alphabetical) so two
    surveys diff without spurious churn. WI-jozap is the standing reminder that
    arbitrary iteration order is a measurement hazard, not a cosmetic one.
  * A run with NO silence_reason is not counted. Its absence means NOT
    APPLICABLE; folding that into the counts at the reporting layer would undo
    the not-applicable / cannot-determine split the field exists to draw.

No schema change: the field already exists, this adds only a reader.

Verified END TO END on a real apollo-server survey rather than by inspection --
the line appears and its numbers reconcile with the JSON exactly (52 + 5 = 57 of
78 pass-runs). Tests: 5,511 passed, 100% coverage on all changed files.

